## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design admission-time governance patterns, CRD design, and distributed state handling in a multi-tenant cluster.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes admission webhooks and CRDs
- Distributed rate limiting (token bucket)
- Cross-replica consistency and RBAC
- Failure modes and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: CRD spec (JS-like for illustration)
class ApiRatePolicySpec {
  constructor(ns, maxRPS, burst) {
    this.namespace = ns
    this.maxRPS = maxRPS
    this.burst = burst
  }
}
```
```javascript
// Webhook skeleton (illustrative)
async function Admit(req) {
  const policy = lookupPolicy(req.namespace)
  if (!policy) return allow()
  const tokens = acquireToken(req.namespace, policy)
  return tokens ? allow() : deny("quota exceeded")
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test webhook scaling and Redis failover?
- What are the trade-offs of fail-closed vs fail-open during webhook outages?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable governance mechanism spanning policy storage, reconciliation, and enforcement across many namespaces, while preserving auditability and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Namespace-scoped policy governance
- Central policy store with versioning
- Auto-generation of RBAC Roles, ResourceQuota, and LimitRange
- Real-time enforcement via admission webhook
- Drift detection and safe rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: reconcile NamespaceGuardPolicy and apply RBAC/limits across a namespace
async function reconcile(ns) {
  const policy = fetchPolicy(ns);
  if (!policy) return;
  ensureRBAC(ns, policy.rbac);
  ensureQuota(ns, policy.quota);
  ensureLimits(ns, policy.limits);
  audit(ns, policy.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this at scale across thousands of namespaces?
- How would you handle policy propagation latency and eventual consistency?
